  "findings": "Structural root-cause tests suggest that slow flow times are driven more by exception/rework activities than by object complexity such as number of items, products, package items, or employees. Slow orders have much higher flow time than median orders and include payment reminders, while median orders do not. Slow packages are strongly associated with failed deliveries: failed delivery is 4.84x more prevalent in slow packages than median packages. Slow items show moderately higher item out-of-stock and reorder activity, each 1.64x more prevalent than in median items. Correlation tests do not support item count, package item count, employee count, or product count as likely drivers of flow time: orders->items, packages->items, and packages->employees correlations are near zero and statistically insignificant; items->products is undefined because every item has exactly one product. The only strong significant structural correlation is customers->orders, but this is at customer level and likely not the primary order-to-delivery bottleneck mechanism.",
